/* Sec+ Gym Cards — part d12 (Domains 1 & 2) */
export const CARDS_D12 = [
 /* ---------- controls (22) ---------- */
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Technical control — the category",a:"#Implemented by systems and software\nFirewalls, encryption, ACLs, antivirus, IDS",s:"Enforced by hardware or software",g:"ctrlcat",
  x:"Technical (sometimes called logical) controls are enforced by technology rather than by people or paper: firewalls, encryption, access control lists, endpoint protection and intrusion detection. On the exam, if the control is a configuration or a device that enforces a rule automatically, it is technical. Categories describe WHO or WHAT applies the control; types (preventive, detective and so on) describe what the control does.",
  w:"Technical = a machine enforces it."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Managerial control — the category",a:"#Policies, oversight and risk decisions\nRisk assessments, security policies, vendor reviews",s:"Policies and risk-management oversight",g:"ctrlcat",
  x:"Managerial (administrative) controls are the governance layer: policies, standards, risk assessments, security planning and management review. They shape how other controls are chosen and funded rather than blocking an attack directly. Exam signal: 'policy', 'risk assessment', 'oversight' or 'management approves' points to managerial, while day-to-day tasks performed by staff point to operational.",
  w:"Managerial = managers decide on paper."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Operational control — the category",a:"#Performed by people day to day\nAwareness training, guard patrols, backup procedures, change procedures",s:"Carried out by people as routine procedures",g:"ctrlcat",
  x:"Operational controls are executed primarily by people rather than systems: security awareness training, incident response procedures, configuration management practices, backups run by staff and guard rotations. The distinction the exam tests is people versus technology: an IPS rule is technical, while the analyst reviewing logs each morning is operational. Managerial controls define the policy; operational controls carry it out.",
  w:"Operational = people operate it every day."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Physical control — the category",a:"#Protects facilities and hardware\nFences, locks, bollards, badges, guards, lighting",s:"Protects buildings and equipment physically",g:"ctrlcat",
  x:"Physical controls limit physical access to facilities, rooms and equipment: fencing, bollards, mantraps (access control vestibules), locks, badge readers, lighting, cameras and guards. SY0-701 lists physical as its own category alongside technical, managerial and operational. Exam signal: anything that stops a person's body rather than a packet — 'server room', 'data center door', 'vehicle' — is physical.",
  w:"Physical = you can touch it (or trip over it)."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Preventive control — the type",a:"#Stops the incident before it happens\nFirewall rules, locks, least privilege, hardening",s:"Blocks the unwanted action from occurring",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"A preventive control acts before an event, blocking the action outright: a firewall dropping traffic, a locked door, disabling unused accounts, patching. The exam contrasts it with deterrent (discourages but does not physically stop) and detective (only notices afterwards). When the question asks what would have 'stopped the attack from succeeding', choose preventive.",
  w:"Prevent = the wall; deter = the warning sign on the wall."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Deterrent control — the type",a:"#Discourages the attempt\nWarning signs, visible cameras, login banners, guards on show",s:"Discourages an attacker from trying",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"A deterrent control lowers the likelihood of an attack by making the attacker reconsider, without actually blocking it: warning signage, a visible (even dummy) camera, a legal login banner, an acceptable-use policy with stated penalties. On the exam, 'discourage', 'psychological' or 'make the attacker think twice' signals deterrent. A guard can be both deterrent and preventive, so read for what the question emphasizes.",
  w:"Deterrent works on the mind, not the door."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Detective control — the type",a:"#Identifies an incident during or after\nIDS, log review, CCTV recordings, audits, motion sensors",s:"Discovers that an incident has happened",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"Detective controls do not stop an event; they find it, ideally quickly. Examples are intrusion detection systems, SIEM alerts, log reviews, audit trails, motion sensors and camera footage reviewed after the fact. Exam signal: 'identify', 'discover', 'alert' or 'after the fact' points to detective. Note that an IDS is detective while an IPS is preventive because it blocks.",
  w:"Detect = the smoke alarm, not the sprinkler."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Corrective control — the type",a:"#Fixes or reverses the damage\nRestoring from backup, patching after exploit, IPS quarantine",s:"Restores systems after an incident",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"A corrective control reduces the impact of an incident that has already occurred and returns things to normal: restoring from backups, re-imaging a compromised host, removing malware, applying an emergency patch. The exam phrase 'after a breach, restore operations' or 'minimize the damage already done' signals corrective. Compare with recovery in some frameworks, but SY0-701 uses corrective.",
  w:"Correct = fix what broke."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Compensating control — the type",a:"#Alternative when the primary control is not feasible\nSegmentation and monitoring around a system that cannot be patched",s:"Stand-in that offsets a control you cannot apply",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"A compensating control provides comparable protection when the intended control cannot be implemented, such as isolating a legacy medical device on its own VLAN with strict monitoring because the vendor forbids patches. Exam signal: 'legacy', 'cannot be patched or upgraded', 'not feasible', then 'what should be implemented instead'. It is not weaker by definition; it is simply a substitute route to the same risk reduction.",
  w:"Compensate = make up for what you cannot do."},
 {obj:"1.1",c:"controls",q:"Directive control — the type",a:"#Tells people what to do\nPolicies, procedures, signage that mandates behavior, training",s:"Instructs or mandates required behavior",g:"ctrltype",
  x:"Directive controls are new in SY0-701 and direct behavior toward compliance: an acceptable-use policy, a mandatory procedure, a sign reading 'authorized personnel only'. Unlike deterrent controls, which discourage bad behavior with consequences, directive controls prescribe the correct behavior. Exam signal: 'policy requires', 'employees must', 'guidance' or 'standard operating procedure'.",
  w:"Directive = directions; deterrent = threat of penalty."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"CIA triad — the three goals",a:"#Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability\nNo unauthorized disclosure, no unauthorized change, reachable when needed",s:"Confidentiality, integrity, availability",v:"cia",
  x:"The CIA triad names the three properties every control protects: confidentiality (only authorized parties can read data — encryption, access control), integrity (data is unaltered — hashing, signatures) and availability (systems are reachable when needed — redundancy, backups). Exam signal: 'leak' or 'eavesdropping' is a confidentiality failure, 'unauthorized changes' or 'tampering' is integrity, and 'outage' or 'denial of service' is availability. Ransomware encrypting files attacks availability.",
  w:"Leak = C, tamper = I, outage = A."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Non-repudiation",a:"#Sender cannot deny having sent it\nProvided by digital signatures with a private key only the signer holds",s:"Proof of origin the sender cannot deny",
  x:"Non-repudiation is the assurance that a party cannot later deny performing an action, most commonly delivered by a digital signature created with the signer's private key. Symmetric MACs do not give non-repudiation because both parties share the key and either could have produced the tag. Exam signal: 'prove who sent it', 'cannot deny', 'legally attributable'."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"AAA — the three functions",a:"#Authentication, Authorization, Accounting\nProve identity, grant permissions, log what was done",s:"Identity proof, permissions, and activity logging",
  x:"AAA frameworks such as RADIUS and TACACS+ split access control into three steps: authentication verifies who you are, authorization decides what you may do, and accounting records what you did for billing and audit. Identification (claiming a username) precedes authentication. Exam questions often ask which A a scenario illustrates: a log of session duration is accounting; a denied file access is authorization.",
  w:"Who are you, what may you do, what did you do."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Gap analysis",a:"#Compare current state to the desired state\nIdentifies missing controls before a project or audit",s:"Measures the gap between current and target posture",
  x:"A gap analysis assesses where an organization's security currently stands against a target such as a framework, regulation or internal baseline, producing a list of shortfalls and a remediation plan. It is typically done before a zero trust rollout, a compliance audit or a new policy. Exam signal: 'determine what is missing', 'compare against the framework', 'before implementing' points to gap analysis rather than a risk assessment."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Zero trust control plane — its components",a:"#Policy engine, policy administrator, adaptive identity\nDecides whether each request is allowed",s:"Policy engine and administrator that make access decisions",v:"zerotrust",
  x:"In the zero trust model the control plane is the decision-making layer: adaptive identity evaluates who is asking and in what context, threat scope reduction limits what they could reach, policy-driven access control applies the rules, and the policy engine plus policy administrator produce and communicate the verdict. Exam signal: 'decides' or 'policy' belongs to the control plane; 'enforces' belongs to the data plane."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Zero trust data plane — its components",a:"#Policy enforcement point, implicit trust zones, subject/system\nEnforces the decision on actual traffic",s:"Policy enforcement point that applies decisions to traffic",v:"zerotrust",
  x:"The data plane is where subjects and systems actually communicate and where the policy enforcement point (PEP) allows or blocks each session based on the control plane's decision. Implicit trust zones are the small areas where traffic is trusted after passing the PEP. On the exam, 'the component that opens or closes the connection' is the PEP in the data plane, while 'the component that evaluates identity and risk' is the policy engine in the control plane.",
  w:"Control plane thinks, data plane does."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Physical sensors — the four exam types",a:"#Infrared, pressure, microwave, ultrasonic\nDetect body heat, weight, or movement to trigger alarms",s:"Infrared, pressure, microwave, ultrasonic detectors",
  x:"SY0-701 names four sensor types used in physical security: infrared detects body heat, pressure sensors detect weight on a floor or mat, microwave sensors bounce radio waves to detect motion, and ultrasonic sensors do the same with sound. They are detective physical controls that feed alarms or cameras. Exam questions may describe the technology ('detects a change in heat signature') and ask for the sensor name or the control type."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"controls",q:"Honeypot, honeynet, honeyfile, honeytoken",a:"#Deception technology\nDecoy systems and data that lure attackers and reveal their methods",s:"Decoy assets that lure and expose attackers",
  x:"Deception and disruption technology plants attractive fakes: a honeypot is a decoy host, a honeynet is a whole decoy network, a honeyfile is a bait document such as 'passwords.xlsx', and a honeytoken is a fake credential or record whose use is an unmistakable alert. Because no legitimate user should touch them, any access is a high-fidelity indicator. Exam signal: 'observe attacker techniques' or 'lure' points here rather than to an IDS.",
  w:"Honey attracts flies; nobody legitimate eats it."},
 {obj:"1.3",c:"controls",q:"Backout plan",a:"#Steps to reverse a change if it fails\nRequired in every change request",s:"Predefined rollback if a change goes wrong",
  x:"A backout (rollback) plan documents exactly how to return a system to its previous state if an approved change causes problems, including restoring configurations or backups. Change management also requires impact analysis, test results, a maintenance window and stakeholder approval. Exam signal: 'the update broke production, what should have been prepared beforehand' points to the backout plan rather than the maintenance window."},
 {obj:"1.3",c:"controls",q:"Impact analysis in change management",a:"#Assesses what a change could affect before approval\nDependencies, downtime, security posture",s:"Evaluates side effects of a change before approval",
  x:"Impact analysis is performed before a change is approved to identify which systems, users and business processes could be affected, whether dependencies such as legacy applications will break, and what security implications exist. It informs the approval decision and the scheduling of a maintenance window. Exam signal: 'before implementing, determine which other services rely on this server' is impact analysis, not a backout plan."},
 {obj:"1.3",c:"controls",q:"Allow list vs deny list",a:"#Allow: only listed items run; deny: listed items blocked\nAllow lists are more restrictive and secure",s:"Allow list permits known-good; deny list blocks known-bad",
  x:"An allow list (formerly whitelist) permits only explicitly approved applications, addresses or senders and blocks everything else, giving strong control at the cost of administration. A deny list (blocklist) blocks known-bad items and allows everything else, which is easier but cannot stop unknown threats. Exam signal: 'only approved software may execute' means allow list; 'block this malicious domain' means deny list.",
  w:"Allow list = guest list; deny list = banned list."},
 {obj:"1.3",c:"controls",q:"Version control",a:"#Tracks every change to files and configs\nEnables rollback and accountability for changes",s:"Tracks change history so revisions can be rolled back",
  x:"Version control systems record who changed what and when for code, scripts, infrastructure definitions and documents, allowing teams to compare revisions, audit changes and revert to a known-good state. SY0-701 lists it under change management technical implications alongside documentation updates. Exam signal: 'restore the previous configuration' or 'identify who modified the script' points to version control.",
  w:"Git remembers so you do not have to."},

 /* ---------- crypto (30) ---------- */
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Symmetric encryption",a:"#One shared secret key\nSame key encrypts and decrypts — fast, but the key must be delivered securely",s:"Single shared key for both encrypt and decrypt",v:"symasym",
  x:"Symmetric algorithms such as AES use the same key for encryption and decryption, so they are very fast and suited to bulk data, but the key must reach the other party over a secure channel and every pair of communicators needs its own key. On the exam, 'fast', 'bulk data', 'shared secret' or 'key distribution problem' signals symmetric. TLS uses asymmetric methods to agree on a symmetric session key.",
  w:"Sym = same key on both sides."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Asymmetric encryption",a:"#Public/private key pair\nEncrypt with the public key, decrypt with the private key; slow but solves key exchange",s:"Public key encrypts, private key decrypts",v:"symasym",
  x:"Asymmetric cryptography uses mathematically linked key pairs: anything encrypted with the public key can be decrypted only with the matching private key, and signatures made with the private key are verified with the public key. It is far slower than symmetric encryption, so it is used to exchange session keys and to sign, not to encrypt large files. Exam signal: 'no prior shared secret', 'public key', 'digital signature'.",
  w:"Public locks the box; only private opens it."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"AES",a:"#Symmetric block cipher, 128/192/256-bit keys\nThe standard for encrypting data at rest and in transit",s:"Symmetric block cipher with 128-256-bit keys",
  x:"The Advanced Encryption Standard is a symmetric block cipher operating on 128-bit blocks with 128, 192 or 256-bit keys, used in full-disk encryption, WPA2/WPA3, TLS and VPNs. AES-GCM adds authentication and is the common modern mode. Exam signal: any question asking for the strongest widely used symmetric algorithm, or which cipher secures wireless or disk encryption, points to AES; DES and 3DES are the deprecated ancestors."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"RSA",a:"#Asymmetric algorithm based on factoring large primes\nUsed for key exchange and digital signatures, 2048-bit keys typical",s:"Asymmetric, relies on difficulty of factoring primes",
  x:"RSA derives its security from the difficulty of factoring the product of two large primes, and supports both encryption and digital signatures with the same key pair. Common key sizes are 2048 and 4096 bits; keys under 2048 are considered weak. Exam signal: 'factoring', 'public key certificate', 'signs the certificate' commonly indicates RSA, while 'smaller key, same strength' indicates ECC."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"ECC — elliptic curve cryptography",a:"#Asymmetric with much smaller keys\n256-bit ECC roughly equals 3072-bit RSA; ideal for mobile and IoT",s:"Asymmetric with small keys, ideal for low-power devices",
  x:"Elliptic curve cryptography achieves strength comparable to RSA with far shorter keys and less computation, which reduces power and bandwidth requirements. It underpins ECDHE key exchange and ECDSA signatures in modern TLS. Exam signal: 'mobile device', 'IoT', 'limited processing power', 'smallest key for equivalent security' points to ECC over RSA.",
  w:"ECC = efficient curves for constrained devices."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Diffie-Hellman",a:"#Key agreement over an insecure channel\nBoth sides derive the same shared secret without ever sending it",s:"Derives a shared secret without transmitting it",
  x:"Diffie-Hellman is a key exchange protocol: two parties exchange public values and each combines them with a private value to compute an identical shared secret that an eavesdropper cannot reconstruct. It does not encrypt data or authenticate parties by itself, which is why TLS pairs it with certificates. Exam signal: 'agree on a session key', 'in-band key exchange', 'ephemeral (DHE/ECDHE)' for perfect forward secrecy."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Key length — why longer is stronger",a:"#Each added bit doubles the brute-force keyspace\n128-bit AES is still secure; 1024-bit RSA is not",s:"Longer keys exponentially increase brute-force effort",
  x:"Key length sets the size of the keyspace an attacker must search: a 128-bit key has 2^128 possibilities, and every extra bit doubles the work. Bit counts are not comparable across algorithm families, so 256-bit ECC is roughly as strong as 3072-bit RSA, and a 128-bit symmetric key is far stronger than a 1024-bit RSA key. Exam signal: 'increase resistance to brute force' means longer keys; 'quantum' concerns favor larger symmetric keys and post-quantum algorithms."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Hashing — the core property",a:"#One-way, fixed-length fingerprint\nSame input always gives the same digest; cannot be reversed",s:"One-way fixed-length digest of any input",v:"hash",
  x:"A cryptographic hash function turns input of any size into a fixed-length digest that changes completely with a one-bit input change, and cannot be reversed to recover the input. Hashes verify integrity, store passwords and feed digital signatures; they are not encryption because there is no key and no decryption. Exam signal: 'verify the file was not altered', 'fixed length output', 'one-way'.",
  w:"Hash = fingerprint, not a lockbox."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"SHA-256",a:"#Secure Hash Algorithm producing a 256-bit digest\nCurrent standard; MD5 and SHA-1 are deprecated",s:"256-bit digest, the current recommended hash",v:"hash",
  x:"SHA-256 is a member of the SHA-2 family that outputs a 256-bit (64 hex character) digest and is used in TLS certificates, code signing, blockchains and file verification. SHA-1 and MD5 have practical collision attacks and should not be used for signatures or certificates. Exam signal: 'which hash should replace MD5' or 'a 64-character hexadecimal checksum' points to SHA-256."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"MD5 — why it is deprecated",a:"#128-bit hash with known collisions\nStill seen for non-security checksums, never for signatures",s:"128-bit hash broken by collision attacks",v:"hash",
  x:"MD5 produces a 128-bit digest and can be attacked so that two different inputs yield the same hash, allowing a forged document or certificate to carry a valid-looking signature. It survives only for quick, non-adversarial file integrity checks. Exam signal: a 32-character hex value is MD5, and a question asking why it should not secure certificates or passwords expects the answer 'collisions'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Salting a password hash",a:"#Random value added before hashing\nMakes identical passwords hash differently and defeats rainbow tables",s:"Random per-user value added before hashing",
  x:"A salt is a unique random string stored alongside each password hash and mixed into the input, so two users with the same password have different digests and precomputed rainbow tables become useless. Salts are not secret; their power is uniqueness. Exam signal: 'rainbow table', 'precomputed hashes', 'identical hashes reveal identical passwords' points to missing salt.",
  w:"Salt makes every hash taste different."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Key stretching (PBKDF2, bcrypt)",a:"#Deliberately slow hashing with many iterations\nRaises the cost of every guess in an offline brute-force attack",s:"Slows hashing thousands of times to resist brute force",
  x:"Key stretching runs a password through a hash function thousands of times (PBKDF2) or uses a purposely expensive algorithm (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) so each guess takes measurable time; a user notices nothing, but an attacker testing billions of candidates is slowed enormously. It is applied in addition to salting. Exam signal: 'increase the work factor', 'iterations', 'strengthen a weak password before use as a key'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"HMAC",a:"#Hash combined with a shared secret key\nProves both integrity and that the sender knew the key",s:"Keyed hash proving integrity and authenticity",
  x:"A hash-based message authentication code mixes a secret key into the hash computation, so a recipient with the same key can verify both that the message is intact and that it came from someone holding the key. Because the key is shared, HMAC gives authentication but not non-repudiation. Exam signal: 'integrity and authenticity with a shared secret' means HMAC; 'sender cannot deny' means a digital signature."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Digital signature — how it is made",a:"#Hash the message, encrypt the hash with the private key\nRecipient decrypts with the public key and compares hashes",s:"Hash signed with the sender's private key",v:"sig",
  x:"To sign, the sender hashes the message and encrypts that digest with their private key; the recipient decrypts the signature with the sender's public key, hashes the received message and checks both digests match. This proves integrity (hash matches), authenticity (only the private key could have signed) and non-repudiation. Exam signal: 'private key signs, public key verifies' — the reverse of encryption for confidentiality.",
  w:"Sign with private, verify with public."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Certificate authority (CA)",a:"#Trusted issuer that signs certificates\nVouches that a public key belongs to a named entity",s:"Trusted third party that issues and signs certificates",v:"pki",
  x:"A certificate authority verifies an applicant's identity and control of a domain, then signs a certificate binding the applicant's public key to that identity. Clients trust the certificate because they already trust the CA's own certificate in their root store. Exam signal: 'who signs the certificate', 'trusted third party', 'browser shows a warning because the issuer is not trusted'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Root of trust and the offline root CA",a:"#Root CA self-signs and stays offline\nIntermediates issue day-to-day certificates",s:"Self-signed root kept offline, intermediates do the work",v:"pki",
  x:"In a PKI hierarchy the root CA's self-signed certificate is the anchor of trust and is kept offline so its private key cannot be stolen; one or more intermediate (subordinate) CAs signed by the root issue end-entity certificates. If an intermediate is compromised it can be revoked without replacing every root store. Exam signal: 'protect the root key', 'chain of trust', 'why use subordinate CAs'.",
  w:"Root sleeps in a vault; intermediates work the counter."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Certificate signing request (CSR)",a:"#Request sent to a CA containing the public key and identity\nThe private key never leaves the requester",s:"Public key plus identity details sent to the CA",v:"pki",
  x:"To obtain a certificate, the requester generates a key pair, keeps the private key, and packages the public key with subject details (common name, organization, SANs) into a CSR signed with the private key to prove possession. The CA validates the details and returns a signed certificate. Exam signal: 'what is sent to the CA', 'the private key should never be transmitted', 'first step to obtain a certificate'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"CRL vs OCSP",a:"#CRL: downloaded list of revoked serials; OCSP: real-time query\nOCSP is faster and current but adds a request per check",s:"CRL is a periodic list; OCSP is a live status query",
  x:"A certificate revocation list is a CA-signed file listing revoked certificate serial numbers that clients download periodically, so it can be stale and large. The Online Certificate Status Protocol lets a client ask the CA's responder about one certificate in real time. Exam signal: 'most current revocation status' means OCSP; 'offline check' or 'published list' means CRL; 'reduce load on the responder and protect privacy' means OCSP stapling."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"OCSP stapling",a:"#Server attaches a fresh CA-signed OCSP response to its TLS handshake\nClient need not contact the CA",s:"Server includes its own signed revocation proof",
  x:"With OCSP stapling the web server periodically fetches a time-stamped OCSP response for its own certificate and includes it in the TLS handshake, so the client verifies revocation status without a separate round trip to the CA. It speeds up connections, reduces load on the OCSP responder and stops the CA from learning which sites users visit. Exam signal: 'improve handshake performance while still checking revocation'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Wildcard vs SAN certificate",a:"#Wildcard: *.example.com covers one level of subdomains\nSAN: lists several distinct names on one cert",s:"Wildcard covers subdomains; SAN lists separate names",
  x:"A wildcard certificate has an asterisk in the leftmost label and secures every single-level subdomain such as mail.example.com and www.example.com, but not example.com itself or deeper names, and its one private key is shared by every host. The subject alternative name extension instead lists explicit hosts or domains, so one certificate can cover example.com and example.org. Exam signal: 'many subdomains, one cert' means wildcard; 'several different domain names' means SAN."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Key escrow",a:"#Copy of a private key held by a trusted third party\nAllows recovery if the owner loses the key or leaves",s:"Trusted third party stores a copy of the private key",
  x:"Key escrow deposits a copy of an encryption key with an internal recovery agent or external escrow service so that encrypted data remains recoverable after key loss, employee departure or a legal order. It supports availability but creates a high-value target that must be strictly controlled with M-of-N access. Exam signal: 'recover encrypted files after the employee left' or 'law enforcement access' points to escrow."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Full-disk vs file vs database vs record encryption",a:"#Levels of encryption granularity\nFDE protects a lost laptop; record-level protects individual fields on a live server",s:"Granularity from whole disk down to a single field",
  x:"Full-disk (or volume) encryption protects everything on a drive but only while powered off, so it defends against theft, not against a logged-in attacker. File-level encryption protects specific files even when the system is running; database and record (column or field) encryption protect data inside a live application so that a DBA or a SQL injection sees ciphertext for sensitive columns. Exam signal: 'laptop stolen' means FDE; 'protect credit card numbers in the table' means record or column level."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"TPM vs HSM",a:"#TPM: chip on the motherboard for one device; HSM: dedicated appliance for many keys\nBoth store keys in tamper-resistant hardware",s:"TPM is per-device chip; HSM is a shared key appliance",
  x:"A Trusted Platform Module is a chip built into a laptop or server that stores keys and measurements for that single machine, enabling BitLocker, secure boot attestation and device identity. A hardware security module is a separate, often network-attached or PCIe device that generates, stores and uses keys for many systems, such as a CA or a payment gateway, with high-speed crypto operations. Exam signal: 'disk encryption key on a laptop' means TPM; 'protect the CA private key' or 'high-volume signing' means HSM."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Secure enclave",a:"#Isolated processor region for keys and biometrics\nEven a compromised OS cannot read its contents",s:"Isolated CPU area shielding keys from the OS",
  x:"A secure enclave is a hardware-isolated area within a processor or system-on-chip that runs its own microkernel and stores biometric templates, encryption keys and payment tokens so that the main operating system, even if rooted, cannot extract them. It is common in mobile devices. Exam signal: 'fingerprint data on the phone is protected from malware on the OS' indicates a secure enclave rather than a TPM."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Key management system (KMS)",a:"#Central service to generate, rotate, store and retire keys\nOften cloud-based; enforces lifecycle and access policy",s:"Central lifecycle management for cryptographic keys",
  x:"A KMS provides a single administered place to create keys, distribute them to applications through APIs, rotate them on schedule, audit their use and destroy them at end of life, frequently backed by an HSM. Cloud providers offer managed KMS services that integrate with storage and database encryption. Exam signal: 'manage keys for hundreds of cloud workloads' or 'automate key rotation' points to a KMS."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Steganography",a:"#Hides data inside another file, such as an image or audio\nConceals that a message even exists",s:"Hides a message inside an innocent-looking file",
  x:"Steganography embeds secret data in the least significant bits of an image, audio or video file, or in unused fields, so that a casual observer sees only a normal file. It provides obfuscation, not encryption; combining both hides the message and protects it if found. Exam signal: 'exfiltrated data inside picture files', 'hide the existence of communication' points to steganography rather than encryption.",
  w:"Stego hides the message; crypto scrambles it."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Tokenization vs data masking",a:"#Tokenization swaps a value for a non-sensitive token mapped in a vault\nMasking replaces characters, e.g. ****-1234",s:"Token replaces the value; masking obscures the display",
  x:"Tokenization substitutes sensitive data such as a card number with a random token; the real value lives only in a secure token vault, so systems handling tokens fall out of PCI DSS scope. Masking hides part of a value for display or test data (showing only the last four digits) and is generally not reversible. Exam signal: 'reduce PCI scope' or 'reversible via a vault' means tokenization; 'show only last four digits on screen' means masking."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Blockchain / public ledger",a:"#Chained hashed blocks replicated across many nodes\nAltering a record breaks every later hash, making tampering evident",s:"Hash-chained distributed record resistant to tampering",
  x:"A blockchain is an append-only distributed ledger in which each block contains the hash of the previous block, so changing any earlier entry invalidates all subsequent hashes and is rejected by the other nodes. It provides integrity and transparency without a central authority, and is used for cryptocurrency, supply chain tracking and audit trails. Exam signal: 'immutable record shared among parties who do not trust each other'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"TLS handshake — what it accomplishes",a:"#Authenticates the server, agrees a cipher suite, derives a session key\nThen bulk data is encrypted symmetrically",s:"Authenticates server and sets up a symmetric session key",
  x:"During the TLS handshake the client and server agree on protocol version and cipher suite, the server presents its certificate which the client validates against trusted CAs, and both sides perform key exchange (usually ECDHE) to derive symmetric session keys for the actual data. Exam signal: 'asymmetric to set up, symmetric to transfer', 'why is the certificate needed', 'which step happens first'."},
 {obj:"1.4",c:"crypto",q:"Perfect forward secrecy",a:"#Ephemeral session keys not derived from the server's long-term key\nA stolen private key cannot decrypt recorded past sessions",s:"Ephemeral keys protect past sessions if the key leaks",
  x:"Perfect forward secrecy is achieved with ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (DHE or ECDHE), generating a fresh key pair per session and discarding it afterwards, so compromise of the server's certificate private key later does not expose previously captured traffic. Static RSA key exchange lacks this property. Exam signal: 'attacker recorded traffic and later obtained the private key' — PFS prevents decryption.",
  w:"Forward secrecy: yesterday's chats stay secret tomorrow."},

 /* ---------- ports (16) ---------- */
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"SSH (Secure Shell)",a:"#22\nEncrypted remote shell and tunnel; replaces Telnet",s:"22",
  x:"SSH provides encrypted command-line access, file transfer (SCP/SFTP) and port forwarding over TCP 22, using public-key or password authentication. It is the secure replacement for Telnet (23) and the cleartext r-commands. On the exam, any question about securely administering a Linux server or network device remotely, or about which insecure port to close after enabling SSH, is pointing at 22 versus 23."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"HTTPS (HTTP over TLS)",a:"#443\nWeb traffic protected by TLS; replaces HTTP on 80",s:"443",
  x:"HTTPS wraps HTTP in TLS on TCP 443, giving confidentiality, integrity and server authentication via certificates. It is the target when a question says 'users see a not secure warning' or 'encrypt traffic to the web application'. Remember the pairing: HTTP 80 is the insecure version to disable. Many other services also tunnel over 443 to pass firewalls, which matters for egress filtering questions."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"LDAPS (LDAP over TLS)",a:"#636\nEncrypted directory queries; plain LDAP is 389",s:"636",
  x:"LDAPS secures directory lookups and binds with TLS on TCP 636, protecting usernames and passwords that plain LDAP on 389 would send in cleartext. Alternatively, StartTLS can upgrade a 389 connection. Exam signal: 'directory authentication traffic is visible in a capture' or 'secure queries to Active Directory' points to 636."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"FTPS (FTP over TLS)",a:"#989/990\nFTP with explicit or implicit TLS; not the same as SFTP",s:"989/990",
  x:"FTPS adds TLS to the classic FTP protocol, using TCP 990 for the control channel and 989 for data in implicit mode (explicit mode negotiates TLS on the standard 21). It keeps FTP's separate data connections, which complicates firewalls. Exam signal: 'FTP with certificates' or 'TLS' means FTPS on 990; 'over SSH' means SFTP on 22."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol)",a:"#22 (over SSH)\nFile transfer inside an SSH session; single port, firewall-friendly",s:"22 (over SSH)",
  x:"SFTP is not FTP with encryption but a distinct file-transfer subsystem running inside SSH on TCP 22, so it uses one connection, SSH keys and SSH host authentication. It is usually the preferred secure replacement for FTP because it is simple to firewall. Exam signal: 'secure file transfer using existing SSH keys' or 'single port' points to SFTP; 'X.509 certificate' points to FTPS."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"SMTPS / SMTP submission with TLS",a:"#587 (STARTTLS) / 465 (implicit TLS)\nAuthenticated client mail sending; server-to-server SMTP is 25",s:"587 / 465",
  x:"Mail clients submit outgoing messages on TCP 587 with STARTTLS upgrading to encryption, or on 465 with TLS from the start. Port 25 remains for server-to-server relay and is commonly blocked outbound to stop spam bots. Exam signal: 'securely send email from a client' means 587 or 465; 'mail relay between servers' means 25."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"IMAPS (IMAP over TLS)",a:"#993\nEncrypted mailbox access that keeps mail on the server; plain IMAP is 143",s:"993",
  x:"IMAP lets a client read and manage mail that stays on the server, synchronizing folders across devices; IMAPS on TCP 993 wraps it in TLS. Plain IMAP on 143 exposes credentials. Exam signal: 'mail remains on the server and is accessed from multiple devices, securely' points to 993; download-and-delete behavior points to POP3S 995."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"POP3S (POP3 over TLS)",a:"#995\nEncrypted mail download to one client; plain POP3 is 110",s:"995",
  x:"POP3 downloads messages to a single client and typically deletes them from the server; POP3S on TCP 995 adds TLS. It is older and less flexible than IMAP but still appears on the exam. Signal: 'retrieve mail securely to a single workstation' points to 995; the insecure counterpart is 110."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol)",a:"#3389\nWindows graphical remote access; never expose directly to the internet",s:"3389",
  x:"RDP on TCP 3389 provides a full graphical session to Windows hosts and is a favorite target for brute force and ransomware operators. Hardening means placing it behind a VPN or gateway, enforcing MFA and network level authentication, and restricting source addresses. Exam signal: 'many failed logons on 3389 from the internet' or 'exposed remote desktop' identifies RDP."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"ports",q:"RADIUS",a:"#1812/1813\nUDP; 1812 authentication, 1813 accounting; only the password is encrypted",s:"1812/1813",
  x:"RADIUS is the AAA protocol used by wireless controllers, VPNs and 802.1X switches to authenticate users against a central server, using UDP 1812 for authentication/authorization and 1813 for accounting (legacy 1645/1646). It encrypts only the password field. Exam signal: 'centralized authentication for Wi-Fi or VPN users' means RADIUS; 'device administration with per-command authorization' means TACACS+."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"ports",q:"TACACS+",a:"#49\nTCP; encrypts the entire payload; separates authentication, authorization and accounting",s:"49",
  x:"TACACS+ is Cisco's AAA protocol on TCP 49, encrypting the whole packet body and splitting the three AAA functions so administrators can be authorized per command on routers and switches. RADIUS combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password. Exam signal: 'granular control of network device administration' or 'entire payload encrypted' points to TACACS+."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"SNMPv3",a:"#161/162\nUDP; 161 queries to agents, 162 traps to the manager; v3 adds authentication and encryption",s:"161/162",
  x:"SNMP manages and monitors network devices: the manager polls agents on UDP 161 and agents send unsolicited traps to UDP 162. Versions 1 and 2c use cleartext community strings; SNMPv3 adds user authentication and encryption and is the only version to use. Exam signal: 'community string public' indicates a legacy version to upgrade; 'device sends alert to the monitoring server' is a trap on 162."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"Syslog and syslog over TLS",a:"#514 / 6514\nUDP 514 is cleartext; TLS syslog uses TCP 6514",s:"514 / 6514",
  x:"Syslog forwards log messages from hosts and devices to a central collector or SIEM, traditionally over UDP 514 with no encryption or delivery guarantee. Secure deployments use TLS on TCP 6514 so logs cannot be read or altered in transit. Exam signal: 'ensure log data is protected on the way to the SIEM' points to 6514; the default 514 is the insecure baseline."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"DNS and DNS over TLS (DoT)",a:"#53 / 853\nUDP/TCP 53 is cleartext resolution; DoT encrypts on TCP 853",s:"53 / 853",
  x:"DNS resolves names on UDP 53 (TCP 53 for zone transfers and large responses) in cleartext, exposing browsing to eavesdropping and spoofing. DNS over TLS on TCP 853 encrypts queries between client and resolver; DNS over HTTPS does the same on 443. DNSSEC, by contrast, signs records for integrity but does not encrypt. Exam signal: 'prevent ISP from seeing DNS queries' means DoT/DoH; 'prevent forged responses' means DNSSEC."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ports",q:"IPsec IKE / NAT-T",a:"#500 / 4500\nUDP 500 for IKE key negotiation; 4500 for NAT traversal",s:"500 / 4500",
  x:"IPsec VPNs negotiate security associations with Internet Key Exchange on UDP 500, and when a NAT device sits in the path they encapsulate ESP in UDP 4500 (NAT-T). ESP itself is IP protocol 50 and AH is 51, not ports. Exam signal: 'site-to-site VPN fails through the firewall' means open UDP 500 and 4500 and permit ESP."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"ports",q:"Kerberos",a:"#88\nTicket-based SSO authentication used by Active Directory",s:"88",
  x:"Kerberos on TCP/UDP 88 authenticates users to a Key Distribution Center, which issues time-limited tickets so passwords never cross the network and users get single sign-on to domain resources. It requires clocks to be synchronized within about five minutes. Exam signal: 'tickets', 'KDC', 'Windows domain logon' or 'authentication fails because of time skew' points to Kerberos."},

 /* ---------- actors (14) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Nation-state actor / APT",a:"#Government-backed, highly resourced, persistent\nMotivated by espionage, disruption, political or military goals",s:"Government-funded, patient, espionage-driven",
  x:"Nation-state actors are sophisticated, well-funded teams that pursue long-term access (advanced persistent threat) for intelligence gathering, sabotage of critical infrastructure or influence operations, and rarely care about quick profit. Exam signal: 'foreign government', 'months of undetected presence', 'custom zero-day malware', 'critical infrastructure' points to a nation-state rather than organized crime.",
  w:"APT: Advanced, Persistent, and paid by a Treasury."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Organized crime actor",a:"#Profit-driven criminal group, well funded\nRansomware, fraud, credential theft, extortion",s:"Financially motivated criminal enterprise",
  x:"Organized crime groups run cybercrime as a business, with developers, affiliates and money-laundering arms, and pick targets by expected payout rather than politics. They are external, highly capable and increasingly offer ransomware-as-a-service. Exam signal: 'demand payment', 'sell stolen card data', 'ransomware affiliate' or 'financial gain' points to organized crime."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Hacktivist",a:"#Ideologically motivated, often external and public\nDefacement, DDoS, doxing to make a statement",s:"Attacks to promote a political or social cause",
  x:"Hacktivists attack to advance a philosophical or political agenda, favoring visible techniques such as website defacement, denial of service and leaking embarrassing data. Skill and funding vary widely, from loose collectives to skilled individuals. Exam signal: 'protest', 'cause', 'message left on the website' or 'targeted because of the company's public stance' points to hacktivism."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Insider threat",a:"#Current or former employee or contractor with legitimate access\nMay be malicious or merely careless",s:"Trusted person misusing authorized access",
  x:"An insider already has credentials, physical access and knowledge of where valuable data lives, so traditional perimeter defenses do not stop them. Motivations include revenge, financial gain, blackmail or simple negligence. Exam signal: 'disgruntled employee', 'recently terminated', 'used their own account to copy data' or 'unintentional exposure by staff' points to an insider; controls include least privilege, monitoring and offboarding.",
  w:"The badge is already inside the building."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Unskilled attacker (script kiddie)",a:"#Low skill, uses pre-built tools and exploits\nMotivated by curiosity, bragging rights or mischief",s:"Low-skill user of downloaded attack tools",
  x:"Unskilled attackers rely on tools, scripts and exploit kits written by others and typically lack the ability to adapt when a target is patched or hardened, so basic hygiene defeats them. They still cause damage through volume and opportunism. Exam signal: 'downloaded a tool from a forum', 'no understanding of how it works', 'wide untargeted scanning' points to an unskilled actor."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Threat actor attributes",a:"#Internal vs external; resources/funding; sophistication/capability\nUsed to profile who is likely behind an attack",s:"Origin, funding level, and skill level",
  x:"SY0-701 profiles actors on three attributes: whether they are internal or external to the organization, how much funding and resources they command, and how sophisticated their capabilities are. Nation-states score high on funding and capability; script kiddies low on both; insiders are defined by being internal. Exam signal: a question listing 'well funded, highly skilled, external' expects you to name the actor type that matches."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Threat actor motivations",a:"#Data exfiltration, espionage, financial gain, disruption, revenge, beliefs\nAlso blackmail, service disruption, war",s:"Why an actor attacks: money, espionage, revenge, ideology",
  x:"The exam lists motivations including data exfiltration, espionage, service disruption, blackmail, financial gain, philosophical or political beliefs, ethical hacking, revenge, disruption/chaos and war. Matching motivation to actor is common: espionage suggests nation-state, financial gain suggests organized crime, beliefs suggest hacktivists, revenge suggests an insider. Exam signal: read the stated goal first, then pick the actor."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"actors",q:"Shadow IT",a:"#Technology deployed without IT approval or knowledge\nUnmanaged cloud apps, personal devices, rogue servers",s:"Unapproved systems set up outside IT's control",
  x:"Shadow IT arises when departments or individuals adopt SaaS tools, storage or devices without security review, creating assets that are unpatched, unmonitored and outside backup and data-protection policy. It is listed as a threat actor because the well-meaning employee unknowingly expands the attack surface. Exam signal: 'marketing set up its own file-sharing account', 'discovered an unknown server on the network'."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Attack surface",a:"#Sum of all points where an attacker could get in\nOpen ports, exposed services, users, suppliers, physical access",s:"Total set of entry points an attacker could use",
  x:"The attack surface is everything reachable by a potential attacker: listening services, web applications, APIs, user accounts, email, wireless, physical premises and third-party connections. Reducing it (closing ports, removing unused software, limiting accounts) is a core hardening goal. Exam signal: 'reduce exposure', 'minimize the number of ways in' points to attack-surface reduction; a specific path used is a threat vector."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Message-based and image-based threat vectors",a:"#Email, SMS, instant messaging carry links, attachments and malicious images\nImages can hide scripts or exploit parsers",s:"Email, SMS, IM and malicious image files",
  x:"Message-based vectors deliver phishing links, malicious attachments and social engineering through email, SMS and chat platforms, and remain the leading initial access path. Image-based vectors embed malicious code in image files or use SVG with scripts, exploiting viewer software or hiding data via steganography. Exam signal: 'employee received a text with a link' or 'a crafted image file crashed the viewer and ran code'."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Removable media and file-based vectors",a:"#USB drives and malicious documents\nAutorun, keystroke-injection devices, macro-laden files",s:"USB devices and malicious documents or executables",
  x:"Removable media vectors include USB drops in parking lots, devices that pose as keyboards to inject commands, and infected drives carried across air gaps. File-based vectors use documents with macros, PDFs with exploits, or trojanized installers. Controls are USB blocking, disabling autorun and macros, and sandboxing attachments. Exam signal: 'found a flash drive and plugged it in' or 'enable macros to view content'."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Unsupported systems and unsecure networks",a:"#End-of-life software and open or weakly protected Wi-Fi, wired, Bluetooth\nNo patches and no encryption invite attack",s:"End-of-life software and open wireless or wired access",
  x:"Unsupported (legacy or end-of-life) systems no longer receive security patches, so every new vulnerability stays exploitable; they need isolation or replacement. Unsecure networks include open Wi-Fi, WEP or WPA, unprotected switch ports and discoverable Bluetooth, allowing eavesdropping and rogue access. Exam signal: 'vendor ended support', 'coffee shop wireless', 'anyone can plug into the lobby jack'."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Open service ports and default credentials",a:"#Unneeded listening services and unchanged factory passwords\nThe easiest wins for automated scanners",s:"Unnecessary listening services and factory passwords",
  x:"Every open port is a service that can be probed and exploited, so hardening disables anything not required and firewalls the rest. Default credentials on routers, cameras, IoT devices and databases are published online and are tried by botnets automatically. Exam signal: 'scan showed Telnet and FTP listening' or 'device still used admin/admin' points to these vectors, fixed by service removal and credential changes."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"actors",q:"Supply chain vectors: MSP, vendor, supplier",a:"#Compromise a trusted third party to reach its customers\nSoftware updates, hardware, managed service access",s:"Attacking through a trusted provider or product",
  x:"Supply chain attacks exploit the trust an organization places in managed service providers with remote access, software vendors whose updates are installed automatically, and hardware suppliers whose components may be tampered with before delivery. One breach at the provider reaches many downstream victims. Exam signal: 'malicious update from a legitimate vendor', 'MSP credentials used to reach clients', 'counterfeit network hardware'.",
  w:"Trust the vendor, inherit the vendor's breach."},

 /* ---------- social (14) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Phishing",a:"#Fraudulent email that lures a click or credential entry\nMass, untargeted, impersonates a trusted brand",s:"Mass deceptive email fishing for clicks or credentials",
  x:"Phishing sends bulk email posing as a bank, vendor or service to trick recipients into opening attachments or entering credentials on a fake page. It is the most common initial access technique and is countered by filtering, DMARC, training and MFA. Exam signal: a generic message to many users with a spoofed sender and a link. Targeted versions have their own names: spear phishing (specific person) and whaling (executive)."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Spear phishing vs whaling",a:"#Spear: targeted at a specific person or group\nWhaling: targeted at executives or high-value individuals",s:"Targeted phishing; whaling aims at executives",
  x:"Spear phishing uses research about the victim (role, colleagues, projects) to craft a convincing message aimed at one person or team; whaling is spear phishing against a CEO, CFO or other senior target whose approval unlocks money or data. Exam signal: 'personalized details' means spear phishing; 'addressed to the CFO' or 'executive' means whaling."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Vishing and smishing",a:"#Vishing: voice call scam; smishing: SMS text scam\nSame deception, different channel",s:"Phone-call and text-message phishing",
  x:"Vishing uses phone calls, often with spoofed caller ID and urgency ('your account is locked'), to extract credentials or payments; smishing delivers the lure by SMS with a link or a number to call. Both bypass email filters. Exam signal: 'received a call claiming to be the help desk' is vishing; 'text message about a package delivery with a link' is smishing.",
  w:"V = voice, Sm = SMS."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Business email compromise (BEC)",a:"#Attacker uses or spoofs a real executive or vendor mailbox\nRequests wire transfers, payroll changes or invoice payment",s:"Impersonated exec or vendor requests a payment",
  x:"BEC targets finance and HR staff with messages that appear to come from a compromised or look-alike executive or supplier account, asking for an urgent wire transfer, gift cards or updated bank details for an invoice. There is often no malware, only social pressure. Exam signal: 'CFO emailed accounting to pay a new vendor account urgently' points to BEC; controls are out-of-band verification and dual approval."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Pretexting",a:"#Invented scenario that justifies the request\nAttacker plays a role: auditor, technician, new hire",s:"Fabricated story used to justify a request",
  x:"Pretexting builds a believable backstory before asking for information or access, such as an 'IT contractor' who needs the Wi-Fi password to finish a ticket, or a 'bank fraud officer' verifying account details. It underpins many vishing and impersonation attacks. Exam signal: 'claimed to be', 'explained they were', 'gave a plausible reason' points to pretexting rather than simple phishing."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Watering hole attack",a:"#Compromise a site the target group already visits\nVictims are infected while browsing normally",s:"Infects a legitimate site the targets frequent",
  x:"Rather than approaching victims directly, the attacker identifies a website an organization or industry relies on, such as a trade association or supplier portal, and plants malware there. Members visiting the trusted site are compromised, often via drive-by download. Exam signal: 'multiple employees infected after visiting an industry news site' indicates a watering hole.",
  w:"Poison the pond the herd drinks from."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Brand impersonation and typosquatting",a:"#Fake sites or messages mimicking a trusted brand\nTyposquatting registers look-alike domains like examp1e.com",s:"Look-alike brands and misspelled domains",
  x:"Brand impersonation copies logos, email templates and login pages so victims trust a fraudulent message or site; typosquatting (URL hijacking) registers domains that differ by a typo, swapped letter or different TLD to catch mistyped traffic or lend credibility to phishing. Exam signal: 'users landed on paypa1.com' or 'the email looked exactly like the bank's' points here; defensive domain registration helps."},
 {obj:"2.2",c:"social",q:"Misinformation vs disinformation",a:"#Misinformation: false info spread without intent to deceive\nDisinformation: deliberately false to manipulate",s:"Unintentional falsehood vs deliberate deception",
  x:"Misinformation is inaccurate content shared by people who believe it; disinformation is crafted and distributed on purpose, often by nation-states or hacktivists, to influence opinion, damage reputation or cover an attack. Both are listed as human vectors in SY0-701. Exam signal: 'coordinated campaign of fake news' is disinformation; 'employee reposted a false claim' is misinformation."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Ransomware",a:"#Encrypts files and demands payment for the key\nOften exfiltrates data first for double extortion",s:"Encrypts data and demands payment",g:"malware",
  x:"Ransomware encrypts files or entire systems and displays a ransom demand, frequently after stealing copies of the data to threaten publication. It attacks availability and is usually delivered by phishing, RDP brute force or a trojan. Defenses are offline backups, patching, segmentation and EDR. Exam signal: 'files renamed with a strange extension and a payment note' is ransomware."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Trojan",a:"#Malware disguised as legitimate software\nUser installs it willingly; often opens a backdoor or RAT",s:"Malicious code hidden in a wanted program",g:"malware",
  x:"A trojan appears to be a useful application, game or update but carries a hidden payload such as a remote access tool, keylogger or downloader. Unlike a virus or worm it does not self-replicate; it depends on the user running it. Exam signal: 'downloaded a free utility, then attackers had remote control' points to a trojan, specifically a RAT."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Worm vs virus",a:"#Worm: self-propagates across the network without user action\nVirus: attaches to a file and needs a user to run it",s:"Worm spreads by itself; virus needs a host file and user",g:"malware",
  x:"A worm exploits network services to copy itself from host to host automatically, causing rapid outbreaks and bandwidth consumption; a virus embeds in a program, document or boot sector and spreads only when that file is executed or shared. Exam signal: 'spread to every unpatched machine overnight with no user interaction' is a worm; 'infected when the user opened the file' is a virus.",
  w:"Worms crawl on their own; viruses hitch a ride."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Spyware, keylogger and bloatware",a:"#Spyware covertly collects data; keylogger records keystrokes\nBloatware is unwanted preinstalled software that widens the attack surface",s:"Covert data collection vs unwanted preinstalled software",g:"malware",
  x:"Spyware monitors browsing, credentials and personal data and reports to the attacker; a keylogger is a hardware or software variant that captures every keystroke to steal passwords. Bloatware is not intentionally malicious but ships preinstalled, consumes resources and often contains vulnerabilities, so hardening removes it. Exam signal: 'credentials stolen despite HTTPS' suggests a keylogger; 'trial software on new laptops' is bloatware."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Logic bomb",a:"#Dormant code that fires on a condition\nA date, an event, or an account being deleted",s:"Malicious code triggered by a specific condition",g:"malware",
  x:"A logic bomb sits inside a legitimate system or script and executes its payload only when a trigger occurs, such as a specific date or the removal of its author's account from payroll. It is closely associated with malicious insiders, especially developers and administrators. Exam signal: 'systems wiped a week after the administrator was fired' or 'script checks the date before running' indicates a logic bomb.",
  w:"Ticking code with a fuse."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Rootkit",a:"#Hides deep in the OS or firmware to conceal itself and other malware\nHard to detect from inside the infected system",s:"Kernel- or firmware-level malware that hides its presence",g:"malware",
  x:"A rootkit modifies the kernel, drivers, bootloader or firmware to intercept system calls, hiding processes, files and network connections from the operating system and most antivirus tools. Detection usually requires booting from clean media or comparing hashes offline; removal often means re-imaging. Exam signal: 'antivirus finds nothing but the system behaves oddly' or 'malware survives OS reinstall' (firmware rootkit)."},

 /* ---------- attacks (14) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Amplified vs reflected DDoS",a:"#Reflected: spoofed source so replies hit the victim\nAmplified: small request produces a much larger reply (DNS, NTP)",s:"Spoofed-source bounce vs response-size multiplication",
  x:"In a reflected attack the attacker sends requests with the victim's spoofed address to third-party servers, which reply to the victim. Amplification chooses protocols whose responses dwarf the request, such as DNS, NTP or memcached, multiplying traffic many times. Most volumetric DDoS combines both. Exam signal: 'traffic arrived from many legitimate DNS servers' or 'large responses to tiny queries'.",
  w:"Reflect = bounce it; amplify = make it bigger."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"DNS poisoning / spoofing",a:"#Forged records in a resolver's cache send users to attacker sites\nDNSSEC signing prevents it",s:"Corrupted cache redirects names to attacker IPs",
  x:"DNS cache poisoning injects false answers into a recursive resolver so every client that queries it is sent to a malicious IP for a legitimate name, enabling credential theft and malware delivery without touching the victim's machine. DNSSEC signs records so forged responses fail validation. Exam signal: 'users typed the correct URL but reached a fake site' or 'whole office affected' points to DNS poisoning."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"On-path attack",a:"#Attacker sits between two parties and relays or alters traffic\nFormerly man-in-the-middle; ARP spoofing, rogue AP, evil twin",s:"Attacker relays and modifies traffic between two hosts",
  x:"An on-path attacker intercepts a session, often via ARP poisoning on a LAN, a rogue access point, or a compromised router, so they can read, modify or inject traffic while both ends believe they are talking directly. TLS with certificate validation, HSTS and VPNs defeat it. Exam signal: 'certificate warning on a public Wi-Fi network' or 'gateway MAC address changed' indicates on-path."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Credential replay",a:"#Captured authentication data resent to gain access\nDefeated by nonces, timestamps, session tokens and MFA",s:"Reusing captured login data or tokens",
  x:"A replay attack captures a valid authentication exchange, password hash, cookie or token and retransmits it later to impersonate the user without ever knowing the password (pass-the-hash is a variant). Protocols resist it with one-time nonces, timestamps and short-lived tokens; MFA and TLS reduce exposure. Exam signal: 'attacker reused a captured session cookie' or 'the same packet seen twice'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"SQL injection",a:"#Malicious SQL inserted through user input\nDumps or alters the database; fix with parameterized queries",s:"Attacker's SQL runs via unvalidated input",
  x:"SQL injection occurs when an application concatenates untrusted input into a database query, letting the attacker append conditions like ' OR 1=1 -- to bypass logins, read tables or drop data. Prevention is parameterized (prepared) statements, input validation and least-privilege database accounts. Exam signal: a single quote, OR 1=1, or 'entire customer table exfiltrated through the login form'. Command injection is the sibling attack where input reaches the OS shell (; && |) instead of the database."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Cross-site scripting (XSS)",a:"#Attacker's script runs in other users' browsers\nStored, reflected or DOM; fix with output encoding",s:"Malicious script executed in victims' browsers",
  x:"XSS occurs when a web application echoes untrusted input into a page without encoding, so a script supplied by the attacker executes in the browser of anyone who views it, stealing cookies or performing actions as them. Stored XSS persists in the site; reflected XSS is delivered via a crafted link. Exam signal: 'script tags in a comment field', 'session cookies stolen from visitors' points to XSS, not CSRF."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)",a:"#Tricks a logged-in browser into sending an unwanted request\nFixed with anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies",s:"Victim's authenticated browser sends attacker's request",
  x:"CSRF exploits the fact that browsers automatically attach session cookies: a malicious page or link causes the victim's browser to submit a request (transfer funds, change email) to a site where they are already authenticated. The site cannot tell it from a legitimate action unless it requires a unique per-request token. Exam signal: 'user was logged into the bank when they clicked the link' and the action happened without their intent.",
  w:"XSS runs code in you; CSRF makes you send requests."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Privilege escalation",a:"#Gaining higher rights than granted\nVertical: user to admin; horizontal: user to another user",s:"Exploiting a flaw to gain higher or other users' rights",
  x:"After initial access, attackers exploit misconfigurations, unpatched kernel bugs or weak service permissions to move from a standard account to administrator or SYSTEM (vertical escalation), or to access peers' data (horizontal). It is a key step before persistence and lateral movement. Exam signal: 'low-privilege web shell became root' or 'user could view another customer's records'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Buffer overflow",a:"#Input larger than the allocated memory overwrites adjacent data\nCan hijack execution; mitigated by ASLR, DEP, bounds checking",s:"Oversized input overwrites memory to hijack execution",
  x:"A buffer overflow writes more data than a fixed-size buffer can hold, corrupting neighboring memory such as the return address so the attacker can redirect execution to injected code. It typically affects C and C++ programs lacking bounds checks. Mitigations include address space layout randomization, data execution prevention and memory-safe languages. Exam signal: 'application crashed after a very long input string'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Race condition (TOC/TOU)",a:"#Outcome depends on timing between check and use\nAttacker changes the resource after it is validated but before it is used",s:"Exploits the gap between checking and using a resource",
  x:"A race condition exists when two operations compete for the same resource and the result depends on their order; time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) is the classic case, where a program verifies a file or balance and an attacker swaps it before the program acts on it. Fixes are atomic operations and locking. Exam signal: 'two withdrawals submitted simultaneously overdrew the account' or 'symlink swapped after the permission check'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Downgrade attack",a:"#Forces use of an older, weaker protocol or cipher\nCountered by disabling legacy versions and using HSTS",s:"Forces a weaker protocol version or cipher",
  x:"A downgrade attack manipulates the negotiation between client and server so they settle on a less secure option the attacker can break, such as TLS 1.0, SSL 3.0 (POODLE) or export-grade ciphers, or from HTTPS to HTTP (SSL stripping). Defenses remove legacy support entirely and use HSTS. Exam signal: 'attacker caused the connection to use an outdated cipher suite'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Password spraying vs brute force",a:"#Spraying: one common password against many accounts\nBrute force: many passwords against one account",s:"Few passwords across many accounts vs many against one",
  x:"Brute force hammers a single account with a large wordlist and quickly triggers lockout; password spraying tries one or two likely passwords (Winter2026!) across every account, staying under lockout thresholds and evading per-account alerts. Detection needs correlation across accounts. Exam signal: 'one failed login per user across the whole domain' is spraying; 'thousands of attempts on the admin account' is brute force.",
  w:"Spray wide and shallow; brute force narrow and deep."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Impossible travel and concurrent sessions",a:"#Logins from distant locations too close in time, or from two places at once\nIndicates stolen credentials",s:"Same account active from geographically impossible locations",
  x:"Impossible travel flags an account authenticating from two locations that no one could physically travel between in the elapsed time; concurrent session usage flags the same account active simultaneously from different devices or regions. Both are strong indicators of credential compromise or session hijacking. Exam signal: 'logged in from New York and then Moscow ten minutes later'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"attacks",q:"Indicators: lockouts, odd logging, resource spikes",a:"#Mass account lockouts, missing or out-of-cycle logs, unexplained CPU or bandwidth use\nEach points to a specific attack pattern",s:"Lockout waves, log gaps, abnormal resource use",
  x:"A surge of account lockouts suggests brute force or spraying; a cleared security log, gaps in events or jobs running at unusual hours (out-of-cycle logging) suggest an attacker hiding tracks or using legitimate tools; sustained CPU, disk or outbound bandwidth spikes suggest cryptomining, a worm or data exfiltration. Exam signal: match the symptom to the cause — 'dozens of unlock tickets', 'log cleared after logon', 'server at 100 percent overnight'."},
];
